  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Physical Form
    
    Aurora Stack is a vertical colony of engineered ice spires carved into the sunlit face of the Berg, its towers rising to catch the aurora's light and channel it downward through networks of prismatic lenses — some the size of a penguin's body, others as intricate as frost-work — into galleries that shift through violet, green, and the pale white-blue that indicates sustained mystic exertion. The architecture is deliberately stratified. Public spaces soar: council halls, trade-floors, and the Lens-Cathedral where consensus proceedings are held under shafts of aurora-light that fall through tiered prisms and strike the ice floor in bands of green and violet. Private spaces compress: residential cells, storage vaults, quarters where fire-core rations are measured in portions that leave flippers shaking.
    
    The ice bears its history visibly. Tool-marks from the original carving remain, some fresh enough to weep meltwater, others sealed with dark seams of fire-core slag imported from the Nightshelf — repair-work that prioritized function over craft. The western face carries a permanent pale dimness in its crystal; creation scripture attributes this to the primordial age, when High-Beak Auditor Selka, the colony's founding figure, concentrated aurora-light toward the eastern galleries. On the foundation-stone, initials carved by Hainou mewei'Frostglass — zhie'Stack's own hand-mark — persist. Someone keeps clearing them of new ice. Graffiti in three dialects marks the lower galleries: merchant-marks, protest-sigils from the Accord years, and one recurring phrase scratched into the Lens-Cathedral's east support pillar: *'The aurora judges; the Exchange counts.'*
    
    The colony's timekeeping is aurora-calibrated, measuring cycles by the light's intensity and spectral shift. This produces persistent discrepancies with the Nightshelf's water-calibrated clocks — a source of administrative friction that has never been resolved and, I suspect, never will be.
    
    ## Sacred Origins
    
    Light Ingjeizhi, reputedly the first structure raised by settlers, channels aurora-light through tiered crystal galleries into foundations older than penguin civilization. I have examined those foundations. The ice-memory impressions in the deepest strata do not correspond to any known penguin activity. Creation scripture holds Light Ingjeizhi as the site where Selka first opened her eyes under the aurora and began to count the world into existence. In the broader Aurora liturgical tradition — the Lens-Cathedral cycle — the Stack itself marks the brow of the dead god, where the first crystallizing light struck the divine body and refracted its last thought into the spires.
    
    These are theological claims, not historical ones, but they shape every institutional decision the colony makes. The aurora is not merely a resource to the Stack's inhabitants; it is grammar. The mystics glow faintly from within — not the warm glow of health, but the pale luminescence of beings slowly crystallizing from the inside out, their robes stiffening with ice-growth, their eyes reflecting light that is not externally present. They speak in measured astronomical metaphors and cannot discuss material scarcity without invoking celestial mechanics. The penguins of the Stack wear flowing white robes and carry themselves with visible consciousness of the aurora's presence: tall and proud in the open galleries where the sky-lights reach them, hunched and hurried in the commercial passages below.
    
    ## Governance and Law
    
    The Stack governs by consensus councils convened only under clear skies — a constitutional requirement that has become a political weapon, since cloud cover now persists longer than it did five winters ago. The Lens-Cathedral serves as the formal chamber for proceedings, confessions, and audits, where confession-ice captures testimony in crystalline precision.
    
    ### Leadership Succession
    
    | Leader | Title | Tenure End | Circumstances |
    |---|---|---|---|
    | Selka | High-Beak Auditor | The Calving | Killed when the eastern face collapsed; her shape was preserved in ice mid-reach toward a door that no longer exists |
    | Xouhoulong | Light-Regent | Post-Calving | Resigned in the aftermath; investigation later revealed his authorization of sealed research into Wake-Singer magic beneath Hearth Plaza |
    | Qeihong | Grand Auditor | Current holder | Inherited the aurora-circlet; governs competing power centers without the institutional weight her predecessors wielded |
    
    The Council of Luminaries adjudicates disputes of broader scope. Luminaries who preside over tribunals are selected by lottery. Thaw-Kept's Section Twelve — originally drafted to authorize defensive action against orca incursions — was invoked to convene an unprecedented cross-cultural tribunal prosecuting orcas under Aurora Stack procedural law for crimes committed in Nightshelf territory. The legal reasoning behind that extension deserves more scrutiny than it has received.
    
    ## Economy and Trade
    
    The Icebound Exchange, the colony's merchant guild, controls trade routes from a crystalline hall where deals are sealed under aurora-light and broken contracts are recorded in ice that never melts. Its symbol — interlocked flippers holding a prism — marks warehouses, ships, and the foreheads of debtors who cannot pay. The Exchange was founded after the Faction Wars when three competing trading families merged rather than destroy each other. The merchant class carries older grudges still: three families — Zhachelang, Lianoshing, and a third long absorbed into corporate genealogy — graduated from poisoning each other's shipments to poisoning the colony's broader economy. On the frost-steps below the spire-yards, fledglings clap out nursery rhymes that encode this history in counting songs about fire-core depletion and trade corruption.
    
    The dependency on Nightshelf fire-cores defines the Stack's material existence. The colony's heating stations require quarterly shipments from volcanic trade hubs such as ☽'slew Ravium~, supply lines now subject to administrative redistribution under emergency Flipper Accord provisions. Merchants speak of the arrangement in bitter jokes: *'We trade light for ash; they trade ash for light; the orcas trade both for blood.'* During the Rofundis∴rupt — the catastrophic collapse of ☽'scar Ramtatus~'s fire-core vein — the Stack sent twelve aurora-crystals to the stricken colony unsolicited. Consensus-approved sympathy, wrapped around a quiet acknowledgment of mutual need.
    
    The upper galleries carry the sharp ozone-tang of aurora-light interacting with carved crystal. The lower levels reek of rendered fat and the metallic-sweet stench of fire-core storage. The smells are inseparable from what they signify: survival and debt, in equal measure.
    
    ## Military History
    
    ### The Shattered-Spire Assault
    
    The eastern ridge was breached during this orca assault. Three terraces collapsed inward. The Stack's time-crystals shattered with the ridge. Evidence later surfaced suggesting the ridge's defenses had been weakened from the inside before the attack — the maintenance corridors assigned as patrol routes to council-tier officials took on darker significance in retrospect. During the final hours, practitioners of the Midnight Claws and ☽'shadow Covens' Order created a fusion of blood-working and fire-knowledge designated Grulkzul Omega Strike. The casting killed all participants, destroyed the eastern ridge, and ended the assault by killing the orca commander Gnarkzund Zaith. It also scored a corruption scar into the ice-memory of the lower terraces that has never been successfully cleansed. Ice-readers who access the site report a persistent frequency matching the hum of the Dreaming Tooth, the orca war-artifact that amplified the assault. The casting was performed without Aurora Stack council approval.
    
    ### The Calving
    
    The eastern face tore open, destroying galleries and flooding the lower levels. High-Beak Auditor Selka was among the dead. Light-Regent Xouhoulong resigned. Grand Auditor Qeihong assumed leadership. The chain of causation — Xouhoulong's authorization of sealed research chambers beneath Hearth Plaza, his secret negotiations with orca clans, the structural stresses these introduced — emerged only through Qeihong's subsequent investigation.
    
    ## Corruption and Deterioration
    
    The Glow-Fissure's corruption has been creeping into the lower spires: ice that will not stay frozen, lenses that cloud with something other than frost, a growing number of penguins whose internal glow has taken on colors the aurora never produces. Hearth Plaza's southern gallery went permanently dark after a Void Matrix assessment cascade — a routine Exchange procedure that stripped seven merchants of their aurora-light. The aurora has not touched the sealed gallery since. Sealed research chambers beneath the plaza, omitted from official schematics, housed experiments into deep-ice pressure-magic that the Stack formally considers taboo. The plaza's crystal geometry, designed to funnel light, also funneled sound — a vulnerability exploited when Wake-Singer chanters propagated hymns through the spires.
    
    An investigation into the Promenade Ward revealed that founding-era ice-memory had been altered through Snowdrift-numb, a meditative technique capable of rewriting frozen impressions. The Ward's memorial wall lists nine settlers lost during the founding's third winter. Independent Nightshelf shadow-ledgers record eleven. The eastern quarter's heat-conductor array holds sixteen fire-cores; only fourteen appear in official logs. These discrepancies — two missing names, two unlogged cores — cast doubt on resource projections derived from founding-era data. The ice-memory here has been made to lie, which is not a thing I write lightly.
    
    ## Succession Crises
    
    Two succession crises have struck the Stack in recent memory, compounding the governance fragmentation.
    
    The death of Veil☽ Glow, the eldest mystic whose aurora-powered arrays regulated temperature across the Stack's inhabited sections, paralyzed the council. Tide-speakers claimed bloodline succession rights; Gift-keepers cited older precedent; recharging adepts disputed authorization chains. Crystal shipments froze. The Pinnacle faction seized the Radiant Aurora-harp — its strings aurora-light pulled taut between crystal tines — from the council chamber and removed it to the Aerie Perch. A compromise leader was eventually chosen. Shipments resumed at sixty percent capacity, preserving the Flipper Accord through necessity rather than conviction.
    
    The death of Luminary Meridian-of-the-First-Light — a Council member found with a crystal shard in her lung at Forum Floe, a major trading venue — opened a second dispute between Claimant Prism-Ascending and Claimant Depth-Keeper, neither of whom held the aurora-seal. Fire-core rationing entered Third Tier: two cores per quarter-cycle residential allotment. Aurora-crystal household allotments fell to two standard crystals per quarter-cycle, with recharging stations at First and Fourth Spire shuttered until the sky cleared. Unregistered shipments through Forum Floe began outnumbering ledgered transactions. The Long Dark approached a Stack whose beacon-fires answered to no settled authority.
    
    ## The Depths and the Vault
    
    Beneath the Stack's lowest levels lies a deep vault untouched by any faction war, equipped with aurora-glass prisms, silver-ice needles, and apparatus for channeling sky-light into crystal. The Compass of Crystal — a navigation artifact originally crafted during the Great Thaw — was reforged there from twelve segments to thirteen, drawing on aurora-light reserves stored within the spires. The upper vaults held sovereignty artifacts including genealogical tomes inscribed in aurora-ink, at least one of which was stolen and became the subject of contested ownership proceedings before the Accord Judiciary.
    
    During the Long Dark, when the boundary between living and dead grows thin, ancestor forms manifest in the trade galleries as translucent figures. High-Beak Auditor Selka's form emerged from the wall of Hearth Plaza during one Long Dark and watched the living for three hours without speaking before the veil drew her back into the ice-memory. The repaired sections of ice hold frozen impressions of the colony's past, and the memory-frost surfaces silhouettes unbidden. The Stack's factional registry records at least one member — Iing zhixai'Ward, an outlawed explorer whose discovery of Thaw-era passages disrupted Nightshelf trade routes — as struck from record by her own colony.
    
    After a Wake-Singer breach at The Fissured Smelt~, a contested Nightshelf forge-site, the Stack enshrined the Thaw-Paths Medallion — a divination artifact — at The Dawn Terrace under Stillness-Bound law. The medallion glows without aurora-light on the anniversary of the breach. Frost-ward Yunjoushong zhu'Eyrie subsequently removed the medallion from Stillness-Bound custody. Its location has since been listed as unverified.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771580329010_0", anchor: "her shape was preserved in ice mid-reach toward a door that no longer exists", text: "The door existed. I found its hinge-marks in the administrative ice three levels below the collapse line. It led to the sealed research chambers — the ones Xouhoulong authorized, the ones the entry discusses under a different heading as though the connection were not immediate. She was not fleeing. She was trying to close them.", type: :correction, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771580329010_1", anchor: "The ice-memory impressions in the deepest strata do not correspond to any known penguin activity", text: "I understated this. The impressions are not merely unattributable — they are structurally unlike anything penguin activity produces. Thermal profiles without fire-cores. Acoustic resonance in frequencies our anatomy cannot generate. Geometric organization in the crystal lattice that implies deliberate arrangement by something that understood ice as a medium, not a material. I recorded what I could and sealed the documentation in the Foundation archive. If the transition proceeds, someone should retrieve it. There will not be a second opportunity to read what is down there.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771580329010_2", anchor: "practitioners of the Midnight Claws and ☽'shadow Covens' Order created a fusion", text: "The heroic casting narrative is the version every faction agreed to remember. Nightshelf operational ice from that period — which I have handled, reluctantly — preserves coordination signals predating the assault by days. The Claws and the Covens did not improvise a fusion under fire. They executed a prepared response. The question of who prepared it, and why the Stack's own council was excluded from the planning, has implications the heroic version was specifically designed to foreclose.", type: :skepticism, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771580329010_3", anchor: "nine settlers lost during the founding's third winter. Independent Nightshelf shadow-ledgers record eleven", text: "Two names. Someone decided two names were worth altering the founding record to erase. I have not been able to recover them. The Snowdrift-numb technique overwrites cleanly — that is its purpose. Whatever those two settlers did or were, it is gone in a way that ordinary death is not.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771580329010_4", anchor: "The ice-memory here has been made to lie, which is not a thing I write lightly", text: "My work assumes the ice is honest. Everything I have catalogued for the Foundation — every impression transcribed, every stratum dated, every cross-reference built — rests on that assumption. If the ice can be rewritten, any of it could be rewritten. I do not believe it has been, widely. But I no longer believe it cannot be.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771580329010_5", anchor: "cloud cover now persists longer than it did five winters ago", text: "Seven winters. The spectral logs are unambiguous. Five is the figure the Exchange publishes.", type: :correction, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771580329010_6", anchor: "Selka's form emerged from the wall of Hearth Plaza during one Long Dark and watched the living for three hours without speaking", text: "Three hours. She watched. She did not speak. One recalls that in the liturgical tradition she is the figure who counted the world into existence — whose voice was the colony's first organizing act. That she returned silent is not a detail the theologians have addressed. I find I cannot address it either.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
